In the just identified homoscedastic model

\begin{equation*}
y =Y\beta_{0}+u ,\qquad Y =Z\pi +v ,
\end{equation*}
put $Z'u$ and $Z'v$ normal and write the IV estimator as

\begin{equation*}
\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta_{0}
     =\frac{Z'u}{\pi Z'Z+Z'v}
     =\frac{Z'u}{\pi Z'Z}\frac{1}{1+Z'v/\pi Z'Z}
     =\frac{Z'u}{\pi Z'Z}-\frac{Z'u\,Z'v}{\pi^{2}(Z'Z)^{2}}+o_{p}\!\left(n^{-1}\right).
\end{equation*}
The first term is mean zero and

\begin{equation*}
E\!\left[\frac{Z'u\,Z'v}{\pi^{2}(Z'Z)^{2}}\right]
      =\frac{n\sigma_{z}^{2}\sigma_{uv}}{\pi^{2}n^{2}\sigma_{z}^{4}}
      =\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\pi^{2}n\sigma_{z}^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Writing the first stage concentration parameter as
$\mu^{2}=n\pi^{2}\sigma_{z}^{2}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$ and
$F=n\pi^{2}\sigma_{z}^{2}/\sigma_{v}^{2}+o_{p}(1)$ we obtain the Nagar
first order bias

\begin{equation*}
E[\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta_{0}]
      \approx-\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\frac{1}{F},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual normalisation $\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$ the
relative bias to the OLS bias is

\begin{equation*}
\frac{{\rm bias}_{IV}}{{\rm bias}_{OLS}}\;\approx\;\frac{1}{F}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $|\,{\rm bias}_{IV}/{\rm bias}_{OLS}\,|\approx F^{-1}$ in the
mean.  In particular $E|\,{\rm bias}_{IV}/{\rm bias}_{OLS}\,|<0.10$ on
average as soon as

\begin{equation*}
F^{-1}\lesssim0.10\iff F\gtrsim 10,
\end{equation*}
which is the usual Staiger–Stock $F>10$ rule of thumb.  The $1/F$
approximation is the mean Nagar bias.  If the $95\%$ upper bound of the
Nagar bias is required the familiar Stock–Yogo weak-instrument
calculation is

\begin{equation*}
{\rm Pr}\!\left\{\,\Big|{\rm bias}_{IV}/{\rm bias}_{OLS}\Big|<0.10
\right\}\approx0.95,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal-relative-bias test.  For one
endogenous regressor and one instrument the Stock–Yogo critical value
is

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}^{{\rm rel}}=104.7\approx 1.0\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}
commonly quoted as $F\approx 100$–$105$, $F\approx 104$–$105$ in the
tables, $F\approx105$ in round numbers, for the $10\%$ maximal relative
bias criterion.  In particular the mean-Nagar $10\%$ relative bias
criterion is $F\approx10$, the $95\%$ upper-bound form of the test
being the $F\approx104$–$105$ Stock–Yogo figure which is often quoted
as the $F>100$ rule for a $10\%$ relative Nagar bias.  This is the
$F\approx10$ mean bias rule, $F\gtrsim 10$ on average, quoted in the
literature as $F>10$ for “bias $<10\%$ of OLS”. ∎

In a just identified homoskedastic model the Nagar mean bias can be written
up to $o(1)$

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\big[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\big]\simeq 
\frac{k_{2}}{\mu^{2}}\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\qquad 
\mu^{2}= \frac{\pi'Z'Z\pi}{\sigma_{v}^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the $k_{2}$ first–stage $F$ the relative bias to the OLS
bias is

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rb}\;\simeq\; \frac{k_{2}}{\mu^{2}}
      \;\simeq\;\frac{1}{F-1}\simeq\frac{1}{F}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $F-1$ being the usual $F-{\rm df_{1}}$ correction.  Put
$k_{2}=1$.  For a given non–centrality $\lambda=\mu^{2}$ 
$F\simeq\chi^{2}_{1}(\lambda)$ and for $k_{2}=1$

\begin{equation*}
W\sim N(\sqrt{\lambda},1),\qquad 
F=W^{2},
\end{equation*}
so that on the $5\%$ one sided lower side

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F}\;\approx\;\sqrt{\lambda}+z_{0.95},\qquad 
z_{0.95}=1.645,
\end{equation*}
up to the negligible $ \Phi(- \sqrt{F}-\sqrt{\lambda})$ term.  The
point Nagar relative bias is $k_{2}/\lambda$.  The $95\%$ one-sided
upper bound for the relative bias is therefore

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rb}_{0.95}\simeq 
\frac{k_{2}}{\big(\sqrt{F}-\,z_{0.95}\big)^{2}}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting ${\rm rb}_{0.95}=0.10$ and $k_{2}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\big(\sqrt{F}-z_{0.95}\big)^{2}= \frac{1}{0.10}=10,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{0.95}}\;\approx\;\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}\approx\big(\sqrt{10}+1.645\big)^{2}\approx
(3.16+1.65)^{2}\approx 2.3\times 10^{1},
\end{equation*}
about $21\!-\!26$ on the usual normal $1.645/1.96$ one/two-sided
convention.  With the $1.645$ one-sided $95\%$ normal quantile this is
$F_{0.95}\simeq 23$, essentially the $F-1\simeq10$ Nagar rule
$F\approx 11$ made robust to the sampling error in the first stage.
In the conventional $F>10$ language this is the $F-1>10$ rule,
$F\approx 11$, i.e. $F\approx 10\!-\!12$ in point form, and $F\approx
16\!-\!26$ on the Stock–Yogo $5\%$ weak–instrument test, the often
quoted Stock–Yogo $10\%$ maximal relative bias critical value for
$k_{2}=1$ being $F_{c}\approx 16.4$, viz. $F\approx 20\!-\!25$ on the
exact non–central $\chi^{2}$ inversion above.  The $F\approx 10$ point
Nagar rule is the $F\to\infty$, $\,{\rm Var}(F)\to0$, limit of the
expression above.

In the $P_{1}\times P_{2}$ cross put $P_{1}=0^{5}$ and $P_{2}=1^{5}$ on the
arbitrary $+$ strand.  With the orientation fixed a gamete which is
formed by a single obligate cross over of the two homologues $X,Y$ is

\begin{equation*}
r_{k}(X,Y)_{i}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
X_{i}& i\le k\\[2mm]
Y_{i}&i>k
\end{array}\right.\qquad 0\le k\le 5 ,
\end{equation*}
so that for the $F_{1}$ heterozygote $X=0^{5},Y=1^{5}$

\begin{equation*}
G_{1}=\Big\{0^{a}1^{\,5-a}\;:\;0\le a\le5\Big\},
\end{equation*}
$|G_{1}|=6$, the $6$ monotone $0\ldots01\ldots1$ haplotypes.  An $F_{2}$
individual is $h_{1}/h_{2}$ with $h_{1},h_{2}\in G_{1}$ and a gamete of
the $F_{2}$ is

\begin{equation*}
r_{k}(h_{1},h_{2})_{i}=
\begin{cases}
h_{1,i}&i\le k\\[1mm]
h_{2,i}&i>k
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
with $h_{j}(i)=0\iff i\le a_{j}$ .  Put $a=a_{1}$, $b=a_{2}$.
Then

\begin{equation*}
r_{k}(i)=0\iff i\le\min (k,a)\;\hbox{ or }\;k<i\le b ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $+$ strand

* $k\le a$ : $r_{k}=0^{b}1^{5-b}\in G_{1}$;
* $k>a$ and $b\le k$ : $r_{k}=0^{a}1^{5-a}\in G_{1}$;
* $k>a$ and $b>k$ :
 $r_{k}=0^{a}1^{\,k-a}0^{\,b-k}1^{\,5-b}$.

Hence apart from the $6$ monotone haplotypes the new $F_{3}$ haplotypes
are exactly the binary words of length $5$ with two interior switches

\begin{equation*}
0^{a}1^{p}0^{q}1^{r},\qquad a+p+q+r=5,\;p\ge1,\;q\ge1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p-1,q-1\ge0$.  Writing $p_{1}=p-1$, $q_{1}=q-1$,
$a+p_{1}+q_{1}+r=3$ gives

\begin{equation*}
N_{2}= {3+4-1\choose 4-1}= {6\choose3}=20
\end{equation*}
non-monotone $F_{3}$ haplotypes.  Together with the $6$ monotone
$0^{a}1^{5-a}$ haplotypes we have

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}=6+20=26
\end{equation*}
different $+$ strand chromosome molecules which can be present in the
$F_{3}$ population, $6$ of them the first-generation $0^{a}1^{5-a}$
mosaics and $20$ of them the $0^{a}1^{p}0^{q}1^{r}$ mosaics with two
switches.  In particular the $6$ monotone haplotypes are always
present and the whole $F_{3}$ haploid repertoire is contained in the
$26$ words above.

Up to ordering of the two homologues this is $N_{h}(N_{h}+1)/2$ diploid
autosomes,
$26\times27/2=351$ distinct diploid genotypes, $26^{2}=676$ ordered
maternal/paternal pairs.  The $26$ haploid words are the $6$ monotone
words and the $20$ words $0^{a}1^{p}0^{q}1^{r}$ with $a+p+q+r=5$,
$p,q>0$, which is the usual $2$–breakpoint repertoire.  This is the
conventional $26$–haplotype count; ignoring the $6$ monotone words the
$20$ genuinely new $F_{3}$ recombinants are the ones normally quoted.
∎

