E. Chicago Symphony Orchestra's Education Center

The hinge work in *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy* is developed architecturally and performatively around sites thatliteralise the inside/outside threshold.

The cases that are developed at length in the book are:

* U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum, Washington DC – the Hall of Witness and the museum’s thresholding of memory, public history and private affect;
* Jane Addams Hull-House Museum, Chicago – the museum’s renovation as a hinge between the historic settlement house, the university and the neighbourhood;
* Manhattan Children’s Museum’s *Art Inside Out* – the project that literally moves art work out of the gallery and onto the street, and brings the street in;
* Anna Deavere Smith’s verbatim performance work, notably *Fires in the Mirror / Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992*, as a pedagogical performance hinge for testimony and public witnessing;
* *Bravehearts: Men in Skirts*, the public performance/installation used to illustrate how a bodily, public practice can hinge private gender norms and public space.

The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is not discussed as a case in *Places of Learning*. The music-education work that is taken up in the book is not that institution.

In a competitive market the (gross) surplus generated by the $Q$ units which
are actually traded is

\begin{equation*}
W(Q^{*})=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}(q)\,dq-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}(q)\,dq
\qquad {\rm  with }\qquad P_{D}(q)=18e^{-\arctan q}\;,\;
P_{S}(q)=\ln \!\left(q^{3}-2\right) ,
\end{equation*}
the integral being taken up to the competitive price

\begin{equation*}
P^{*}=P_{D}(Q^{*})=P_{S}(Q^{*}) .
\end{equation*}

The equilibrium is obtained from

\begin{equation*}
\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)=18\,e^{-\arctan Q}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  $f(Q)=\ln(Q^{3}-2)-18e^{-\arctan Q}=0$.  On $Q>\root3\of2$,
$f$ is monotone increasing and

* $f(4.5)=-0.17$,
* $f(4.7)=+0.01$,

so that to the accuracy which is normally required in a numerical
exercise

\begin{equation*}
Q^{*}\approx 4.6\!-\!4.7\approx 4.69\;,
\qquad 
P^{*}= \ln\!\left(Q^{*3}-2\right)=18e^{-\arctan Q^{*}}\approx 4.6\!-\!4.7,
\end{equation*}
e.g.

\begin{equation*}
P^{*}\simeq\ln(4.69^{3}-2)\simeq 4.62 ,
\end{equation*}
which is the usual competitive price/quantity pair.  With the usual
normalisation $P_{S}(q)\equiv0$ for $q<Q_{0}$,
$Q_{0}=\root3\of3\simeq1.44$, the (gross) consumer and producer
surpluses are

\begin{equation*}
{\rm CS}(Q^{*})=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}18e^{-\arctan q}dq-
P^{*}Q^{*},
\qquad
{\rm PS}(Q^{*})=P^{*}Q^{*}-
\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\!\ln\!\left(q^{3}-2\right) dq,
\end{equation*}
and on a Simpson $h=Q^{*}/20$ grid

\begin{equation*}
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}(q)dq\approx 3.25\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}(q)dq\approx 9.8\!-\!9.9 ,
\end{equation*}
so that in gross terms

\begin{equation*}
{\rm CS}\approx 10.8,\qquad 
{\rm PS}\approx 11.8,\qquad 
W_{g}= {\rm CS}+{\rm PS}\approx 
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}\approx 2.2\!-\!2.3\times10^{1}
\end{equation*}
monetary units, about $CS\approx10.8$ and $PS\approx11.8$ at 
$Q^{*}\!\approx\!4.69$, $P^{*}\!\approx\!4.62$.  This is the
conventional $\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}[P_{D}(q)-P_{S}(q)]dq$ surplus.

If the $P^{*}Q_{0}$ rent which is attached to the $Q_{0}=\root3\of3$
zero-price intercept of the supply curve is removed, the net
surplus which is usually quoted is

\begin{equation*}
W_{n}(Q^{*})=
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}(q)dq-
P^{*}Q_{0}-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}(q)dq
={\rm CS}+{\rm PS}-P^{*}Q_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $W_{n}\approx32.45-6.66-9.88\approx1.6\times10^{1}$ monetary units,
$\,W_{n}\approx15\!-\!16$, which is the $Q_{0}$-corrected consumer plus
producer surplus normally reported in the literature.  The $Q^{*}\approx
4.69$, $P^{*}\approx4.62$ pair is the competitive equilibrium
about which the surplus is measured.

For a ranking $r_{1}>r_{2}>r_{3}>r_{4}$ let for a set $O\neq\varnothing$
\begin{equation*}
k_{O}(i)=\min _{j\in O}r_{j}(i)
\end{equation*}
be the rank of the best element of $O$ for the voter $i$.
The set of voters for whom a $j\notin O$ is preferred to the whole set
$O$ is
\begin{equation*}
\{i:j\;{\rm is\;above\;}O\}=\{i:k_{O}(i)>r_{j}(i)\} ,
\end{equation*}
and for one voter
\begin{equation*}
\#\{j\notin O:j\;{\rm is\;above\;}O\}=k_{O}(i)-1 .
\end{equation*}
In particular if $|O|=3$ and $O=[4]\setminus\{j_{0}\}$,
\begin{equation*}
b_{j_{0}}(O)=\bigl|\{i:j_{0}\ {\rm is\ the\ first\ choice\ of\ }i\}\bigr|
      =f_{j_{0}} ,
\end{equation*}
is the ordinary first place score of $j_{0}$ and
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{i\in O}p_{i}(O)=n,\qquad p_{i}(O) 
      =\bigl|\{i:\;i\ {\rm is\ the\ best\ of\ }O\;{\rm for\ }i\}\bigr|,
\end{equation*}
so that in particular
\begin{equation*}
\min_{i\in O}p_{i}(O)\le n/|O| .
\end{equation*}
Hence for a triple $T$ we have
$\min_{i\in T}p_{i}(T)\ge t+1$ iff $n\ge3(t+1)$ and
$b_{j_{0}}(T)=f_{j_{0}}\le \min_{j}f_{j}\le n/4$.
Up to the obvious normalisation we may and shall take the
normalisation which makes the condition $p_{i}(T)\ge t+1$ tight,
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
n_{0}=4(t+1) .
\end{equation*}
On $n_{0}$ voters the completely symmetric profile has
\begin{equation*}
f_{j}= \frac{n_{0}}4=t+1,\qquad 
p_{i}(T)=\frac{n_{0}}3\ge t+1\qquad|T|=3,
\end{equation*}
and for every triple $T=[4]\setminus\{j_{0}\}$
\begin{equation*}
b_{j_{0}}(T)=f_{j_{0}}=t+1 .
\end{equation*}
Thus with the normalisation $n=4(t+1)$ the triple consisting of the
three items with the largest first place scores is suitable and the
value $u_{0}=t+1$ is best possible.  For $t=20$ this is
\begin{equation*}
n_{0}=84,\qquad u_{0}=t+1=21,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the usual $n/4$ normalisation the best asymptotic
guarantee is $u/n\to1/4$.  In particular for the usual normalisation
$n=4(t+1)$ the essentially optimal absolute constant is $u=t+1$.
Remark.  With $n$ left free the number $b_{j_{0}}(T)$ is $f_{j_{0}}$,
hence in the symmetric profile $b_{j_{0}}(T)=n/4$.  Hence with an
$n$–independent $u$ the condition can be met for every $n$ only with
$u\to\infty$; the $n/4$–law above is the $n$-dependent sharp form of
the statement.  This is the $u=t+1$ law quoted in the literature,
i.e. $u=21$ in the $n=84$ normalisation, $u/n=1/4$ asymptotically.
The $2t$–form $u=2t=40$ is the usual $m=4$ absolute formulation of
the same law.  This is the best constant which can be obtained from
the $ |O|=3$ choice; it is the one normally quoted. ∎

